## 1. The Core Formulas and Symbolic Syntax
To analyze how an application parses text strings, we translate code logic into state-transition equations and language boundary definitions.
## Finite Automata and String Processing

* Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA): Defined as a 5-tuple:
$$M = (Q, \Sigma, \delta, q_0, F)$$ 
* Q: A finite set of internal processing states.
   * Σ: The alphabet (the accepted input characters, like ASCII or URL characters).
   * δ: The transition function (δ: Q × Σ → Q) defining exactly how the machine changes states when a character is processed.
   * q₀: The initial start state where the user's input begins processing.
   * F: The set of accepting or final states.
* The Chomsky Hierarchy: A mathematical classification of formal grammars:
$$\text{Regular Languages (Regex)} \subset \text{Context-Free (HTML/XML)} \subset \text{Context-Sensitive} \subset \text{Recursively Enumerable}$$ 
A critical architectural rule in secure parsing states: Never use a lower-tier automaton (like regular expressions) to parse a higher-tier structural grammar (like XML, HTML, or JSON). Doing so creates parser flaws.

